---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Tape from "../components/Tape.astro";
import Legend from "../components/Legend.astro";
import {
  specs,
  url,
  kib,
  unproven,
  total,
  evidenceUrl,
  evidenceAsset,
  evidenceCitedBy,
} from "../lib/specs";
import { NOTE_GROUPS, notesOfKind } from "../lib/hardware";

const t = specs.tally;
const n = specs.total;
const { unverified, undeclared } = t;
---

<Base title="K230 Verification Ledger">
  <!--
    The count comes first. Everything else on this page is downstream of it:
    the site exists to answer "which of these claims has been observed?"
    before a reader has to ask.
  -->
  <section class="verdict">
    <p class="eyebrow">LILYGO T-Display-K230 &middot; specification ledger</p>

    {
      n === 0 ? (
        <>
          <h1 class="count count-empty">
            <span class="n">0</span>
            <span class="of">requirements published yet</span>
          </h1>
          <p class="lede">
            Nothing has been archived into <code>openspec/specs/</code> yet.
            Capabilities appear here as changes are archived, and each requirement
            arrives either grounded in something observed or marked unverified.
          </p>
        </>
      ) : (
        <>
          <h1
            class:list={[
              "count",
              { "count-clear": unverified === 0 && undeclared === 0 },
            ]}
          >
            <span class="n">{unverified}</span>
            <span class="of">
              of {n} requirement{n === 1 ? "" : "s"}{" "}
              {unverified === 1 ? "is" : "are"} unverified
            </span>
          </h1>
          {undeclared > 0 && (
            <p class="alarm">
              <strong>{undeclared}</strong> more declare{undeclared === 1 ? "s" : ""} no
              status at all &mdash; neither grounded nor marked. The build that
              produced this page failed and named them.
            </p>
          )}
          <p class="lede">
            On this board the documentation and the hardware disagree routinely. A
            datasheet described a working radio whose enable line is never driven. So
            a requirement counts as grounded only when something was observed on the
            board, or vendor source was read and cited by path &mdash; never because a
            datasheet says so.
          </p>
          {unverified === 0 && undeclared === 0 && (
            <p class="lede caveat">
              <strong>Zero unverified is not zero unknown.</strong> A capability
              appears in this list when a change is archived, and only what has
              been archived is counted &mdash; the display, radio and runtime
              groups have nothing here yet, while the work behind them is under
              way. What has been observed and not yet made into a requirement is
              in the bands and notes below, and is not counted anywhere.
            </p>
          )}
        </>
      )
    }

    <Tape tally={t} />
    <Legend tally={t} />
  </section>

  <!--
    2026-09-21, and the newest thing here. It sits above the first-boot band
    because the bands are a log and a log runs newest first. It is also the
    one section on this page describing work that is NOT in the count above,
    which is why it says so in its own body rather than leaving a reader to
    reconcile "the screen works" with "no display requirement exists".
  -->
  <section class="milestone" aria-labelledby="screen-h">
    <p class="milestone-eyebrow">
      <span class="live" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      the screen &middot; 2026-09-21
    </p>
    <h2 id="screen-h">The panel lights, and the touch controller reports.</h2>
    <p class="milestone-lede">
      The AMOLED emits under Linux, the kernel console renders on the glass at
      71&times;77 characters, and the GT9895 delivers multitouch to
      <code>/dev/input/event0</code>. Both faults were the same shape: a driver
      that reported itself healthy while producing nothing. The panel had been
      sent <em>a different panel&rsquo;s</em> initialisation sequence &mdash;
      correctly transcribed, from a function with no callers &mdash; and never
      told to leave zero brightness. The touch controller had its interrupt
      declared edge&#8209;triggered on a part that holds the line low until it
      is acknowledged: 0 interrupts against 2173.
    </p>

    <div class="milestone-shot">
      <a href={evidenceUrl("docs/evidence/panel-photos/05-console-on-panel.jpg")}>
        <img
          src={evidenceAsset("docs/evidence/panel-photos/05-console-on-panel.jpg")}
          alt="The board's AMOLED panel showing the Linux kernel console, photographed off the board."
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </a>
      <p>
        <code>docs/evidence/panel-photos/05-console-on-panel.jpg</code> &mdash;
        photographed off the board, not rendered. Five more photographs are
        committed beside it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ul class="milestone-facts">
      <li>
        <b>51 FE &mdash; SET_DISPLAY_BRIGHTNESS</b>
        <span>
          A DCS&#8209;brightness AMOLED powers up at zero, so it can be fully
          initialised, accept every command, and emit nothing.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>0 &rarr; 2173 interrupts</b>
        <span>
          Same kernel, same image, one device&#8209;tree word:
          <code>IRQ_TYPE_EDGE_FALLING</code> to <code>IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_LOW</code>.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>a patch that blanked it</b>
        <span>
          25% DSI burst headroom, built and never looked at. The persistent
          journal dated the regression to the image. Reverted, remeasured, and
          the brightness flicker largely went with it.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>not in the count above</b>
        <span>
          Requirements enter the count when a change is archived. The display
          work is in flight, and this site does not publish proposals.
        </span>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="milestone-go">
      <a href={url("hardware/panel-lit/")}>
        <b>The photographs, the dead function, and the clock that blanked it</b>
        <span>panel note &rarr;</span>
      </a>
      <a href={url("hardware/touch-reports/")}>
        <b>Why the controller was awake and silent for a day</b>
        <span>touch note &rarr;</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!--
    2026-09-20. The single largest thing that has happened to this project,
    and the only section on this page whose facts came off the board rather
    than out of a file. It sits directly under the count because the count is
    the site's product and this is its best entry.
  -->
  <section class="milestone" aria-labelledby="milestone-h">
    <p class="milestone-eyebrow">
      <span class="live live-past" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      first boot &middot; 2026-09-20
    </p>
    <h2 id="milestone-h">NixOS reached a root shell on the silicon.</h2>
    <p class="milestone-lede">
      Not under emulation, not on the build host. A kernel this repository
      cross-compiled, an image this repository wrote, an SD card in the board
      &mdash; and a prompt on <code>/dev/ttyACM0</code> that answered four
      questions no x86 machine and no <code>qemu-system-riscv64&nbsp;-machine&nbsp;virt</code>
      could have answered the same way.
    </p>

    <pre class="milestone-term" tabindex="0" aria-label="Console transcript from the booted board"><i>[root@nixos:~]#</i> cat /proc/cpuinfo
processor	: 0
hart		: 0
isa		: rv64imafdcv_zicbom_zicboz_zicntr_zicsr_zifencei_zihpm_zba_zbb_zbs_svpbmt
mmu		: sv39
<b>uarch		: thead,c908</b>
<b>mvendorid	: 0x5b7</b>
marchid		: 0x8000000009140d00
mimpid		: 0x50000

<i>[root@nixos:~]#</i> uname -a
<b>Linux nixos 6.6.36 #1-NixOS</b> SMP Tue Jan  1 00:00:00 UTC 1980 <b>riscv64</b> GNU/Linux

<i>[root@nixos:~]#</i> cat /proc/cmdline
console=ttyS0,115200n8 root=fstab loglevel=4 lsm=landlock,yama,bpf <b>init=</b>/nix/store/2h0xrlf5n74fjjhxqd18y6vl55w4zyrr-nixos-system-nixos-26.11.20260919.20b1ddd/init

<i>[root@nixos:~]#</i> findmnt -no SOURCE,FSTYPE /
/dev/mmcblk1p2 ext4

<i>[root@nixos:~]#</i> nproc
1</pre>

    <ul class="milestone-facts">
      <li>
        <b>thead,c908 &middot; 0x5b7</b>
        <span>
          T&#8209;Head&rsquo;s vendor ID and the C908 microarchitecture. This is the
          real K230D, not a host and not <code>virt</code>.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>6.6.36 #1-NixOS</b>
        <span>
          The Xuantie kernel built by this flake, running as a NixOS system
          &mdash; not the vendor image the board shipped with.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>init= arrived intact</b>
        <span>
          <code>/proc/cmdline</code> is exactly the string the SD image writes
          to <code>bootargs.txt</code>. Making that true took a fix in the
          U&#8209;Boot environment.
        </span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>root=fstab resolved</b>
        <span>
          No explicit <code>root=</code>, yet <code>/</code> is
          <code>/dev/mmcblk1p2</code>. The <code>NIXOS_SD</code> label path had
          never been exercised before; it works.
        </span>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="milestone-go">
      <a href={url("hardware/first-boot/")}>
        <b>The full transcript, and why Linux gets one hart</b>
        <span>first-boot note &rarr;</span>
      </a>
      <a href={url("hardware/boot-chain/")}>
        <b>How the kernel command line got through</b>
        <span>boot-chain note &rarr;</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>

  {
    specs.capabilities.length > 0 && (
      <section class="ledger">
        <h2 class="section-title">Capabilities</h2>
        {specs.groups.map((g) => (
          <>
            <h3 class="group">
              {g.name}
              {g.blurb && <span class="group-blurb">{g.blurb}</span>}
              <span class="rule" />
            </h3>
            {g.slugs.map((slug) => {
              const cap = specs.capabilities.find((c) => c.slug === slug)!;
              return (
                <a class="row" href={url(`c/${cap.slug}/`)}>
                  <div class="row-name">{cap.name}</div>
                  <div class="row-purpose">{cap.purposeFirst}</div>
                  <div class="row-tape">
                    <Tape tally={cap.tally} small />
                  </div>
                  <div class="row-count">
                    <strong>{unproven(cap.tally)}</strong>/{total(cap.tally)}
                    <span class="row-count-l">unproven</span>
                  </div>
                </a>
              );
            })}
          </>
        ))}
      </section>
    )
  }

  <section class="hw-links">
    <h2 class="section-title">Hardware notes</h2>
    <p class="defect-lede">
      Hand-authored rather than rendered from the specs, and maintained here
      rather than anywhere else. They are split by where their facts came from,
      because that is what decides how much they are worth: a note written from
      a console transcript outranks a note written from a device tree, and both
      get corrected in place when the repository learns something newer.
    </p>
    {
      NOTE_GROUPS.map((g) => (
        <>
          <h3 class="hw-links-group">
            {g.name}
            <span class="rule" />
          </h3>
          <p class="hw-links-blurb">{g.blurb}</p>
          <ul>
            {notesOfKind(g.kind).map((n) => (
              <li>
                <a href={url(`hardware/${n.slug}/`)}>
                  <b>{n.title}</b>
                  <span>{n.blurb}</span>
                </a>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </>
      ))
    }
  </section>

  {
    specs.defects.length > 0 && (
      <section class="defects">
        <h2 class="section-title">Build defects</h2>
        <p class="defect-lede">
          These stopped the build from succeeding. A requirement with no declared
          status, or one citing evidence that is not committed, is a defect in the
          requirement.
        </p>
        <ul>
          {specs.defects.map((d) => (
            <li>
              <span class="defect-kind">{d.kind}</span>
              <span class="mono">{d.source}</span>
              <span>{d.requirement}</span>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </section>
    )
  }

  {
    specs.evidence.length > 0 && (
      <section class="evidence-index">
        <h2 class="section-title">Evidence on file</h2>
        <p class="defect-lede">
          Committed in the repository and rendered here in full, so a boot log
          or a photograph is readable without a checkout. An observation on the
          board outranks vendor source; a datasheet grounds nothing. The two
          lists are kept apart because they carry different weight.
        </p>

        <h3 class="group">
          grounding a requirement
          <span class="group-blurb">what the count above rests on</span>
          <span class="rule" />
        </h3>
        <ul>
          {evidenceCitedBy("requirement").map((e) => (
            <li>
              <a href={url(`evidence/${e.slug}/`)}>
                <code>{e.path}</code>
              </a>
              <span class="ev-size">{kib(e.bytes)}</span>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>

        {evidenceCitedBy("note").length > 0 && (
          <>
            <h3 class="group">
              cited by a hardware note
              <span class="group-blurb">
                real, and holding up no requirement yet
              </span>
              <span class="rule" />
            </h3>
            <ul>
              {evidenceCitedBy("note").map((e) => (
                <li>
                  <a href={url(`evidence/${e.slug}/`)}>
                    <code>{e.path}</code>
                  </a>
                  <span class="ev-size">{kib(e.bytes)}</span>
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </>
        )}
      </section>
    )
  }
</Base>
